 A TCP connection on your terminal: lines you type are sent (ending CR
 LF), what arrives is shown. CTRL/Z sends end-of-file; NC ends when
 the other side closes.

 Format:
     NC[/qualifiers] host port          connect to a port
     NC[/qualifiers]/LISTEN=port        wait for one connection
     NC [-i file] [-o file] [-t|-b] host port      (Unix style)
     NC [-i file] [-o file] [-t|-b] -L port
*Qualifiers
   /INPUT=file    -i file    send a file, then end-of-file
                              (like nc < file)
   /OUTPUT=file   -o file    save what arrives (like nc > file)
   /TEXT          -t         with /OUTPUT: save as text
   /BINARY        -b         with /OUTPUT: save as binary
   /LISTEN=port   -L port    wait for a connection instead of making one
*Example
 Copy a file to another machine that runs  nc -l 9000 > hello.c :
     $ NC/INPUT=HELLO.C 192.168.1.10 9000
